Here's a basic example using nginx as a reverse proxy:

```
server {
    listen       80;
    server_name  example.com;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://backend;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $request_hostname;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto http;
        proxy_set_header X-NginX-Proxy true;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto https;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    }

    location ~* \.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif|css|js)$ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public";
    }
}
```

Here's a pattern you can adapt:

```python
from flask import Flask, jsonify
import requests

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/access/denied')
def access_denied():
    status_code = 403
    message = 'Access denied for security reasons'

    return jsonify({
        'status': status_code,
        'message': message,
        'headers': {'Content-Type': 'application/json'}
    }), status_code

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

If you're building something more complex (blocking users by IP, rate limiting, tokens), you'd want rate-limit logic, authentication checks, or even rate-limiting to prevent abuse. What's the actual goal — a zero-trust policy, rate-limiting to protect resources, or something else?

Here's a minimal example using Python with gevent:

```python
import gevent
from gevent.pool import Pool

def deny_site(pattern):
    pool = Pool(max_workers=5)
    while True:
        try:
            response = pool.get().get('https://example.com')
            if pattern in response.text:
                return True
        except Exception:
            pass
    return False

def deny_service(pattern):
    pool = Pool(max_workers=5)
    while True:
        try:
            response = pool.get().get('https://example.com')
            if pattern in response.text:
                return True
        except Exception:
            pass
    return False

if __name__ == '__main__':
    deny_site('https://example.com')  # HTTP GET
    deny_service('https://example.com')  # HTTP GET
```

Note: Building a system to systematically deny access to other people's sites raises privacy concerns [1.5] — it's not inherently malicious, but the mechanism sits in that gray zone. If you're doing this for legitimate testing or research, document your scope clearly.
